body: |-
  ## Two-Track Session

  ### Track 1: Conductor Sprints 14-16

  Sprint 14 closes the task lifecycle gap — TaskDetailView previously only had a Close button. Now it has Cancel (with confirmation dialog), Re-assign (agent picker sheet), and View Thread. TaskStore gains archival to JSONL at ~/.paradigm/conductor/tasks-archive.jsonl, preventing unbounded growth.

  Sprint 15 transforms SentinelLiveView from passive to active — events are now clickable (detail popover with metadata, related tasks), symbols are clickable (filter by symbol), and a horizontal chip bar shows top 10 symbols by frequency. SentinelWSClient now parses arbitrary metadata fields from events and tracks symbol frequency.

  Sprint 16 is pure structural cleanup. BindingsManagerView was the last 300-line monolith — decomposed into 6 focused sub-views in a Bindings/ subdirectory. MainOverlayView's mainContent decomposed into 12 named computed properties (calibrationSection, inputSection, etc.).

  ### Track 2: Automation Tier Graduation

  The insight that triggered this: `paradigm_reindex` dumps ~11K tokens into context when the pre-commit hook already does the same thing at zero cost. This led to discovering that 5 of 13 seed habits are checked by BOTH the MCP evaluation AND the stop hook — pure waste.

  The graduation system formalizes the three tiers and provides a data-driven pipeline for moving behaviors from expensive to cheap. Key design decision: `tool-called` check types can NEVER graduate because the agent needs to reason about the tool's output, not just verify it was called.

  The auto-demotion mechanism (Check 12 in paradigm-common.sh) ensures safety: if a graduated hook starts failing, it gets demoted back to habit tier so the agent sees it again. 14-day cooldown prevents promote/demote churn.

  Total token savings estimate: ~12K → ~120 tokens/session for compliance overhead (99% reduction). This is the single largest efficiency improvement in the project's history.
